#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151967 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "511feb42-d0dd-55b6-85c5-f0605fcf8da4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9454" :question/problem "A body of mass $3 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}).$\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =t^2- e^{t-4}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{e}^{t-4}) \\\\  & =2t-{e}^{t-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=4 \\, \\textrm{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =2(4)-{e}^{4-4} \\\\  & =8-1 \\\\  & =7\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (7) \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F| = |21| = 21 \\, \\textrm{N}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151976 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/id #uuid "3ecb82f0-3877-5732-851d-adec73abb890" :question/math-academy-id "e-9859" :question/problem "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t) = e^{3t-6} + t^2$ meters. Find the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t-6}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t-6}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{e}^{3t-6}+2t) \\\\  & =9{e}^{3t-6}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force acting on the body, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot (9{e}^{3t-6}+2) \\\\  & =18{e}^{3t-6}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151977 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/id #uuid "2666ce7a-356a-574a-88fd-97417afc8ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-9860" :question/problem "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ moves in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\geq 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =2t^3-12t^2-30t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time when the force acting on the body has a magnitude of zero." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}-30t+5) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-24t-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-24t-30 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-4t-5 & =0 \\\\ (t+1)(t-5) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t \\geq 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude when $t=5 \\, \\textrm{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b58342de-c454-5afe-9ecb-ccafd98db48e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 5s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149059} {:answer/id #uuid "d43ff081-53ff-551e-9f2a-b0dc4ac8ae7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149060} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b2fc05-bb67-59ca-b877-9588be0e5e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149061} {:answer/id #uuid "1eda97be-7632-50f1-89a9-15ea96362e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 1s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149062} {:answer/id #uuid "7484898e-4401-56e2-b0e3-591df12b1c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 6s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7b2fc05-bb67-59ca-b877-9588be0e5e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149061} :answer-field/id #uuid "977bf223-9796-5239-a7a2-1373f812b958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "396d3531-2c0c-5e02-bb04-58dd2fc7f372" :question/math-academy-id "q-138769" :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-6t-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-6t-36 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t-6 & =0 \\\\ (t-3)(t+2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5048da6-f06e-54ef-99f3-02d35784d53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 7s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149065} {:answer/id #uuid "f141134b-33f4-5535-a223-d62b5f9b2448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149066} {:answer/id #uuid "06ce788d-868d-5cf0-b75c-d08a7ee4d31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 4s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149067} {:answer/id #uuid "55869669-22e0-5ae3-a4b8-017647f5cb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149068} {:answer/id #uuid "60a06afb-6930-5bb6-9a10-096b29ce56c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 5s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55869669-22e0-5ae3-a4b8-017647f5cb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149068} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca7066d7-ace6-51fb-8248-23eb546ef0b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8295ff34-59d6-595e-a3a0-97b1c540771c" :question/math-academy-id "q-138770" :question/problem "A body of mass $1.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+t-5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}+t-5) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-24t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-24t+1) \\\\  & =12t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12t-24 & =0 \\\\ 12 & =24 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "964c0021-ca44-5b1a-a9fc-0ee132e37e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310650} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea27fb8-06a3-5a06-87b6-d86b8ba2e9c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310651} {:answer/id #uuid "0c8fcff5-aa5e-5c06-bcf5-6bccd067a1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡t+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310652} {:answer/id #uuid "891e5c26-c31f-5283-843d-844e46211a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡t-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310653} {:answer/id #uuid "d02dea46-58e7-55f5-9bcb-1fcb7cbecfa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡t-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c8fcff5-aa5e-5c06-bcf5-6bccd067a1ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡t+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310652} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b4bad3-27ac-5d5a-8326-972f4ea6f1ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ec922f7-fb5c-5c96-9d82-3da583b98300" :question/math-academy-id "q-138881" :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+2t$ meters per second.\nFind an expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+2t) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf72cbd3-dd94-540a-8362-f3cf1c112f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9t-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310657} {:answer/id #uuid "5135abd7-75f8-59a0-8140-66557a2b50f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{t}^{2}+4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310658} {:answer/id #uuid "08792690-b840-53f0-b1e1-98ba41d33b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{t}^{2}-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310659} {:answer/id #uuid "2dd3b807-c3a0-5055-9b19-42c98da1708b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18t+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310660} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e3f5e6-9cb8-5c0b-83d2-23556169f0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08792690-b840-53f0-b1e1-98ba41d33b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{t}^{2}-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310659} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb889bcc-ca8e-5a05-8908-d31a5d343660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "253cf769-d1eb-5f48-a820-f2091a94ee95" :question/math-academy-id "q-138882" :question/problem "A body of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}$ meters per second.\nFind an expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =6\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-2t) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "878beeac-d61b-5bf0-9a2b-6b41aff66d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24{e}^{2t}-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310664} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5325b3-7241-537f-a326-f60477e4a478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2t}-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310665} {:answer/id #uuid "184eaca3-31bb-5561-8cf6-c0f17fcc7727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{e}^{2t}-18t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310666} {:answer/id #uuid "ccbbd2ae-3131-53d6-a6ef-b221f80640ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{e}^{2t}-6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310667} {:answer/id #uuid "78658f23-26dd-5b83-a1bc-29519a54624a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{e}^{2t}-12t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "184eaca3-31bb-5561-8cf6-c0f17fcc7727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{e}^{2t}-18t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310666} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e3b8fc4-2210-5501-96fa-fe95a42ffe3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce8fb41e-d014-5085-a244-2a0981843cf2" :question/math-academy-id "q-138901" :question/problem "A body of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (4{e}^{2t}-6t) \\\\  & =12{e}^{2t}-18t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2bf88e1-b72c-5cd8-95ad-7210fbda20b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{5}{t\\sqrt{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310671} {:answer/id #uuid "88bf2c30-aa47-50f0-84cc-06d8c4f1c600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5t\\sqrt{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310672} {:answer/id #uuid "caf49b0e-746b-56f6-bc08-dcf38b07a5a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+t\\sqrt{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310673} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba95a31-b049-5f75-95cd-b309a553ef34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t\\sqrt{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310674} {:answer/id #uuid "c093a4cd-6154-558b-880b-dec91848686a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{t\\sqrt{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c093a4cd-6154-558b-880b-dec91848686a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{t\\sqrt{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310675} :answer-field/id #uuid "638b5a0f-668d-502a-9c35-3845bd216efd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d7a503f-e626-542c-b8d1-121fa51ecce9" :question/math-academy-id "q-138902" :question/problem "A body of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=t-4\\sqrt{t}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4\\sqrt{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4{t}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =1-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {t}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =1-2{t}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2{t}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {t}^{-3/2} \\\\  & ={t}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{t\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d6e1ddd-0962-5517-9e06-0e4129354d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310678} {:answer/id #uuid "221bca98-3ad1-5099-b91b-8ffc8f862466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310679} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5b0f1b-2365-53cf-b810-4df5c1706eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310680} {:answer/id #uuid "b90355dd-9a3c-594a-a991-71b9b460256e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310681} {:answer/id #uuid "23547460-662f-5353-83d9-f56dd1cf691e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "221bca98-3ad1-5099-b91b-8ffc8f862466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310679} :answer-field/id #uuid "377ca35b-447c-5135-bbfa-3af2c5c3c412" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "aff0d900-0d16-5502-bd82-5d0e6f780ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-138907" :question/problem "A body of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{2}+{e}^{5-t}$ meters.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=5$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+{e}^{5-t}) \\\\  & =4t-{e}^{5-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-{e}^{5-t}) \\\\  & =4-(-{e}^{5-t}) \\\\  & =4+{e}^{5-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =4+{e}^{5-5} \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =4\\cdot (5) \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F|=|20|=20\\,\\text{N}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7f6738d-d214-53f2-affa-d863550c7c24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310685} {:answer/id #uuid "26060830-13b8-5cdc-8158-83319a96fad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310686} {:answer/id #uuid "0d08b910-cae3-5d0e-9f15-da49bda6e653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=6\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310687} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5b0859-2158-5130-9925-3bcb6cd2ad05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310688} {:answer/id #uuid "3cdaf7d2-2ebd-5e0d-9182-9f571241ec6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26060830-13b8-5cdc-8158-83319a96fad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310686} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdea838b-e469-5954-b6b6-0b08d30a01b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "163b7f1d-5e1e-53cc-9007-34ee797242e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-138911" :question/problem "A body of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-24t+8$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-24t+8) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-6t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-6t-24 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t-8 & =0 \\\\ (t+2)(t-4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dabab117-8064-519b-a9d9-a84ccd69c222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310692} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd5fc9a-309f-5bcd-b9fd-cfd56130e0ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=8\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310693} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f54d68-3b62-5d77-8c54-b3083ceacd08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=6\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310694} {:answer/id #uuid "5e64792e-49cb-52f3-a6c0-6404c989f508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310695} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3ae733-a0ac-5904-8c33-530d472249f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=10\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e64792e-49cb-52f3-a6c0-6404c989f508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310695} :answer-field/id #uuid "8da7164d-0e13-5785-a481-bdbb442ef57a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c703448-496c-5606-b90b-425604c02e8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-138912" :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=6{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}-36t+2$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}-36t+2) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-18t-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18{t}^{2}-18t-36 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t-2 & =0 \\\\ (t+1)(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48747f22-fc52-5e6b-9dbd-8b39acffbaa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310699} {:answer/id #uuid "8753c6f7-b969-5624-b5f3-e1ed6fb9fe62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=8\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310700} {:answer/id #uuid "89e4bb85-0fe1-5c47-baf3-cf7ba0659cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=6\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310701} {:answer/id #uuid "74fba03d-094d-555a-a4c4-d6c42e9a0dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310702} {:answer/id #uuid "175eaa1f-bf3e-5480-9ddb-18f8abe15e62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74fba03d-094d-555a-a4c4-d6c42e9a0dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310702} :answer-field/id #uuid "9556cb54-d20c-5bb3-9707-5df22c1c108b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "142d4d3c-01be-59e8-b6af-dd8e598fcc6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-138916" :question/problem "A body of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={t}^{4}-150{t}^{2}+8$ meters.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-150{t}^{2}+8) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-300t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-300t) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-300\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{t}^{2}-300 & =0 \\\\ 12{t}^{2} & =300 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =25 \\\\ t & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "890ad7ab-f832-5bbd-8703-1bda9d215d3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54e^{2t}\\sin(e^{2t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310706} {:answer/id #uuid "a47e2ea1-5911-5af6-8d9f-388fcde92ccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-54e^{2t}\\sin(e^{2t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a47e2ea1-5911-5af6-8d9f-388fcde92ccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-54e^{2t}\\sin(e^{2t})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310707} :answer-field/id #uuid "db0a69ad-8878-5464-b311-545bda2e2b35" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a37e0cd1-3596-5af2-9194-435d5855f8a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-345208" :question/problem "A body of mass $9\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{2t})$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{2t})] \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2t})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2t})\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =-6{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =9\\cdot [-6{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2t})] \\\\  & =-54{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d6c7ee3-056f-5006-add8-ea0b96bbf603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d6c7ee3-056f-5006-add8-ea0b96bbf603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310710} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0865b0c-fea1-57a3-8ada-e53ed090fe9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc008106-3de7-5b76-a831-099686c669f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-345209" :question/problem "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\frac{2t}{t+1}$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2t}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(t+1)\\cdot (2t)^{′}-2t\\cdot (t+1)^{′}}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)-2t(1)}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2t+2)-2t}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5364993f-c959-5f87-8f90-e78a76d66821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5364993f-c959-5f87-8f90-e78a76d66821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310713} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7afd2ea-b5c1-5328-8032-7541938d6408" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05a4a7a9-8c35-544f-a2e7-da58644256f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-345223" :question/problem "A body of mass $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ meters per second. \n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =ma(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(\\frac{\\pi }{4})|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310714 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42e48301-3781-5b1f-ac80-f45f4f63437f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(t)-t\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42e48301-3781-5b1f-ac80-f45f4f63437f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(t)-t\\sin(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310716} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5b299de-f350-54a7-bf19-2252c4ffc75d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "485f113e-1072-57f0-bd8c-02699b28f536" :question/math-academy-id "q-345339" :question/problem "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce59985-a7f4-5f73-a23f-fdccae5d9d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310719} {:answer/id #uuid "5930bfc1-b505-5eb2-8747-afba73ba7203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5930bfc1-b505-5eb2-8747-afba73ba7203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310720} :answer-field/id #uuid "1869c403-75e3-5b0f-82a1-cdcc46c7da89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e253f00d-234d-5211-a4f0-8165426a2570" :question/math-academy-id "q-345345" :question/problem "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t>0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={e}^{2t}$ meters.\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}) & =4{e}^{2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}]} \\\\  & =4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}) & =ma(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(2)|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$"}]}
